1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,578
A college student picks
up a Laddoo Lab six-pack.

2
00:00:03,578 --> 00:00:05,490
A hundred and twenty rupees?

3
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:06,804
She puts it back.

4
00:00:07,104 --> 00:00:09,945
She loves the taste, but
she cannot spend that much.

5
00:00:10,295 --> 00:00:13,707
Should Zoya lose her, or
charge her a different price?

6
00:00:13,707 --> 00:00:15,520
That is price discrimination.

7
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:21,099
Laddoo Lab sells to busy parents, at a
hundred and twenty rupees a six-pack.

8
00:00:21,449 --> 00:00:25,343
But Zoya's college friends want
laddoos too, and they have less money.

9
00:00:25,693 --> 00:00:28,852
And an office nearby wants
four packs every month.

10
00:00:28,852 --> 00:00:30,677
Can one price fit everyone?

11
00:00:31,327 --> 00:00:33,364
So what is price discrimination?

12
00:00:33,664 --> 00:00:37,741
It means selling the same product to
different buyers at different prices,

13
00:00:37,741 --> 00:00:40,088
even though it costs the same to make.

14
00:00:40,438 --> 00:00:41,937
The word sounds bad.

15
00:00:41,937 --> 00:00:45,478
Here, discrimination simply
means telling buyers apart.

16
00:00:46,128 --> 00:00:48,441
Zoya runs the numbers for one week.

17
00:00:48,791 --> 00:00:51,971
First, one price for everyone:
a hundred and twenty.

18
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:53,711
Fifty parents buy.

19
00:00:53,711 --> 00:00:55,582
Twenty students walk away.

20
00:00:55,932 --> 00:00:59,700
Fifty times a hundred and
twenty is six thousand rupees.

21
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:01,636
Now, two prices.

22
00:01:01,636 --> 00:01:04,169
Parents still pay a hundred and twenty.

23
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:06,856
Students with a college ID pay a hundred.

24
00:01:07,207 --> 00:01:10,552
Fifty parents buy, and now
twenty students buy too.

25
00:01:10,902 --> 00:01:13,520
Twenty times a hundred adds two thousand.

26
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:15,446
Eight thousand rupees in all.

27
00:01:15,796 --> 00:01:20,767
A pack still costs eighty to make, so
each student pack earns twenty rupees.

28
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:21,882
Everyone gains.

29
00:01:22,532 --> 00:01:23,606
Same laddoo.

30
00:01:23,606 --> 00:01:24,957
Different buyers.

31
00:01:24,957 --> 00:01:26,307
Different prices.

32
00:01:27,007 --> 00:01:29,453
But it only works under four conditions.

33
00:01:29,803 --> 00:01:30,703
One.

34
00:01:30,313 --> 00:01:32,420
You can tell the groups apart.

35
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:34,318
Students show a college ID.

36
00:01:34,668 --> 00:01:35,568
Two.

37
00:01:35,198 --> 00:01:37,113
Cheap buyers cannot resell.

38
00:01:37,113 --> 00:01:39,630
One student pack per ID, per day.

39
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:40,880
Three.

40
00:01:40,522 --> 00:01:43,265
The groups really differ
in what they can pay.

41
00:01:43,615 --> 00:01:44,515
Four.

42
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:46,407
It must be legal, and feel fair.

43
00:01:47,057 --> 00:01:51,961
Economist Arthur Pigou described
three degrees of price discrimination

44
00:01:51,961 --> 00:01:52,884
in 1920.

45
00:01:52,884 --> 00:01:55,858
Where do we see them at work today?

46
00:01:56,208 --> 00:01:57,226
First degree.

47
00:01:57,226 --> 00:01:59,481
Each buyer pays a personal price.

48
00:01:59,481 --> 00:02:03,173
A car dealer bargains a
different price with every buyer.

49
00:02:03,523 --> 00:02:04,663
Second degree.

50
00:02:04,663 --> 00:02:07,465
The price depends on how much you buy.

51
00:02:07,465 --> 00:02:10,918
A wholesaler charges less
per box for big orders.

52
00:02:11,268 --> 00:02:12,320
Third degree.

53
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,598
The price depends on your group.

54
00:02:14,598 --> 00:02:17,558
A museum charges students
and children less.

55
00:02:18,208 --> 00:02:20,339
But is price discrimination fair?

56
00:02:20,339 --> 00:02:22,190
It depends how it is done.

57
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:23,583
The good side.

58
00:02:23,583 --> 00:02:28,041
More people can afford the product,
and the business earns enough to grow.

59
00:02:28,391 --> 00:02:29,498
The hard side.

60
00:02:29,498 --> 00:02:31,968
People who pay more can feel cheated.

61
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:35,701
And apps that show each person
a secret price feel unfair.

62
00:02:36,051 --> 00:02:36,951
Mrs.

63
00:02:36,584 --> 00:02:39,222
Rao asks, why do students pay less?

64
00:02:39,222 --> 00:02:44,067
Zoya explains, and offers her the
monthly pack of four, for four hundred

65
00:02:44,067 --> 00:02:45,407
and forty rupees.

66
00:02:45,757 --> 00:02:51,580
In India, the Competition Act of 2002
bans unfair or discriminatory prices

67
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:53,414
by a dominant firm.

68
00:02:53,764 --> 00:02:54,664
The lesson?

69
00:02:54,632 --> 00:02:58,412
Different prices are fine, when
the reason is fair and open.

70
00:02:59,062 --> 00:03:00,023
Quick check.

71
00:03:00,023 --> 00:03:02,817
A cinema sells cheaper
tickets to students.

72
00:03:02,817 --> 00:03:05,711
Which degree of price
discrimination is this?

73
00:03:07,611 --> 00:03:08,607
Third degree!

74
00:03:08,607 --> 00:03:11,116
The price depends on the buyer's group.

75
00:03:11,716 --> 00:03:12,725
So, remember.

76
00:03:13,025 --> 00:03:17,189
Price discrimination means different
prices for different buyers, for the

77
00:03:17,189 --> 00:03:18,154
same product.

78
00:03:18,504 --> 00:03:21,669
It needs groups you can tell
apart, who cannot resell.

79
00:03:22,019 --> 00:03:25,858
Three degrees: by person, by
how much you buy, and by group.

80
00:03:26,458 --> 00:03:29,454
Next time, we'll explore
distribution channels.
